# M4.19 local path discovery

- Time: 2026-08-28 (Europe/Berlin)
- Scope: local, read-only source inspection before M4.19 implementation
- Symptom: the first inspection addressed `src/tu1nz_core/commercial_candidate.py`, which does not exist in the application repository.
- Cause: the M4.17 runtime candidate is located at `src/tu1nz_sandbox/commercial_candidate.py`.
- Impact: none; the failed command was read-only and changed no application or server state.
- Resolution: continue all source inspection against the discovered canonical path and keep the application repository unchanged.
- Rollback: not applicable; this diagnostic note is the only resulting change.

## Follow-up discovery

- A combined follow-up inspection correctly read the application module but addressed Control scripts while still using the application repository as its working directory.
- The resulting missing-file message was read-only and caused no state change.
- Repository-scoped inspection is used from this point forward: application paths below `adult-publishing-core`, Control paths below `control-ssot`.

- A later mixed inspection repeated the same harmless working-directory mistake for one trailing `rg` invocation. The preceding Control reads succeeded; only the application-targeted `rg` paths were absent under Control. No write or runtime action occurred.

## Local systemd verifier availability

- Python AST parsing and Bash syntax validation passed for the new artifacts.
- `systemd-analyze verify` is unavailable on the macOS development host (`command not found`).
- The unit is therefore validated locally by exact static safety assertions. A native `systemd-analyze verify` remains a mandatory read-only check in the separately authorized Linux installation preflight.

## Existing S1 assertion after backup-variable separation

- The first full Control suite found one S1 test failure after the backup script renamed its internal generic dump variables to explicit `S1_*` names.
- The S1 archive paths, database, ownership, mode, upload and notification behavior were not removed or weakened.
- Resolution: update the existing assertion to the explicit `S1_DUMP_DIR` name, then rerun the full suite. No installed backup script or server state is changed.

- The focused rerun found the matching stale `chmod` assertion as well. It also confirmed that an intentionally incomplete M4.19 archive is now rejected earlier with the more specific empty/missing database-dump error. Both are assertion updates; the fail-closed behavior is retained.
